---
import BlogPost from '../../layouts/BlogPost.astro';
import Icon from '../../components/Icon.astro';
import { prices, durations, bonusMonths, connectionTabs } from '../../data/pricing';

const money = (n: number) => `$${n.toFixed(2)} CAD`;
const advice: Record<number, { who: string; text: string }> = {
  1: { who: 'One viewer at a time', text: 'Fits a single person, or a household where only one TV, phone or tablet is ever streaming at once.' },
  2: { who: 'Two screens at once', text: 'Fits a couple or two people with different tastes, for example the living-room TV and a phone or bedroom TV running at the same time.' },
  3: { who: 'Three screens at once', text: 'Fits a small family where two or three people often watch different things in the evening.' },
  4: { who: 'Four screens at once', text: 'Fits a busy household with several TVs and tablets, or anyone who hosts and regularly has multiple screens running together.' },
};
const wa = (n: number) =>
  `https://wa.me/14062356618?text=${encodeURIComponent(`Hello Prime IPTV, I need help choosing between plan lengths for ${n} connection${n === 1 ? '' : 's'}.`)}`;
---
<BlogPost slug="how-many-iptv-connections-do-i-need">
  <p>Prime IPTV plans come with <strong>1, 2, 3 or 4 connections</strong>. The right number saves money if you would have over-bought, and saves frustration if you would have under-bought. Need more than 4? Message us on WhatsApp or email and we will quote a plan for more devices. This guide explains what a connection is and helps you pick.</p>

  <h2>What is an IPTV connection?</h2>
  <p>In IPTV, a <strong>connection</strong> normally means <strong>one stream playing at a time</strong> on your subscription. It is about how many screens are watching <em>at the same moment</em>, not how many devices you have installed the player on. You can set up a player on a Firestick, a phone and a laptop, but with a 1-connection plan only one of them can stream at once.</p>

  <h2>How to count your connections</h2>
  <ol>
    <li>List every screen that might be watching in the evening: living room, bedroom, kids' room, phone, tablet and computer.</li>
    <li>Decide the <strong>most that would run together</strong> on a busy night. That number is your starting point.</li>
    <li>If it is more than 4, <a href="/contact/">contact us</a> about options.</li>
  </ol>
  <p>Also remember the internet limit: every extra simultaneous stream uses bandwidth. As a rough guide, plan for about 10 Mbps per HD stream and 25 Mbps per 4K stream. Several 4K screens at once need a fast connection, so check the <a href="/iptv-internet-speed-requirements/">internet speed requirements</a> and <a href="/blog/iptv-buffering-troubleshooting/">our buffering guide</a>.</p>

  <h2>Quick chooser</h2>
  <div class="chooser" id="chooser">
    <p class="chooser-q"><strong>How many screens might stream at the same time?</strong></p>
    <div class="chooser-btns" role="group" aria-label="Number of simultaneous screens">
      {[1, 2, 3, 4].map((n) => <button type="button" class="cbtn" data-n={n}>{n}</button>)}
      <button type="button" class="cbtn" data-n="5">5 or more</button>
    </div>

    {connectionTabs.map((n) => (
      <div class="cresult" id={`res-${n}`} hidden>
        <h3><Icon name="check" size={20} class="ic-check" /> {n} {n === 1 ? 'connection' : 'connections'}: {advice[n].who}</h3>
        <p>{advice[n].text}</p>
        <p>Prime IPTV plans for {n} {n === 1 ? 'connection' : 'connections'} start at <strong>{money(prices[n][3])}</strong> for 3 months, and go up to <strong>{money(prices[n][24])}</strong> for 24 months (+2 months free).</p>
        <div class="cres-actions">
          <a class="btn-primary" href="/pricing/"><Icon name="card" size={16} />See the {n}-connection plans</a>
          <a class="btn-outline" href={wa(n)} target="_blank" rel="noopener"><Icon name="chat" size={16} />Ask on WhatsApp</a>
        </div>
      </div>
    ))}
    <div class="cresult" id="res-5" hidden>
      <h3><Icon name="info" size={20} class="ic-accent" /> More than 4 screens</h3>
      <p>Standard plans cover up to 4 connections. If you regularly stream on more screens, contact us and we will advise on the best option.</p>
      <div class="cres-actions">
        <a class="btn-primary" href="/contact/"><Icon name="mail" size={16} />Contact us</a>
        <a class="btn-outline" href="https://wa.me/14062356618?text=Hello%20Prime%20IPTV%2C%20I%20need%20more%20than%204%20connections." target="_blank" rel="noopener"><Icon name="chat" size={16} />WhatsApp</a>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>

  <h2>Prices for every plan</h2>
  <p>This is the complete Prime IPTV price list. Monthly figures are the plan price divided by the number of paid months; free bonus months are extra.</p>
  <div class="table-wrap">
    <table id="price-table">
      <thead>
        <tr><th>Plan length</th>{connectionTabs.map((n) => <th data-col={n}>{n} {n === 1 ? 'connection' : 'connections'}</th>)}</tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        {durations.map((m) => (
          <tr>
            <td><strong>{m} months</strong>{bonusMonths[m] > 0 && <><br /><span class="bonus">+{bonusMonths[m]} {bonusMonths[m] === 1 ? 'month' : 'months'} free</span></>}</td>
            {connectionTabs.map((n) => (
              <td data-col={n}><strong>{money(prices[n][m])}</strong><br /><span class="per">{money(prices[n][m] / m)} / mo</span></td>
            ))}
          </tr>
        ))}
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>

  <h2>Which plan length should you pick?</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>3 months</strong> is the lowest commitment and a good way to settle in after the trial.</li>
    <li><strong>6 and 12 months</strong> lower the monthly cost and include +1 month free.</li>
    <li><strong>24 months</strong> has the lowest monthly cost and includes +2 months free.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>Not sure yet? <a href="/free-trial/">Start the 24-hour free trial</a> first (no credit card is required) and use our <a href="/blog/iptv-free-trial-checklist/">trial checklist</a> to test your own setup. A 7-day money-back guarantee also applies, and the <a href="/refund/">refund policy</a> explains how it works.</p>
</BlogPost>

<style>
  .chooser { background:var(--bg-2); border:1px solid var(--border); border-radius:12px; padding:1.5rem; margin:1rem 0 1.5rem; }
  .chooser-q { margin:0 0 0.75rem; color:var(--text); }
  .chooser-btns { display:flex; flex-wrap:wrap; gap:0.5rem; margin-bottom:1rem; }
  .cbtn { background:#fff; border:1.5px solid var(--border); color:var(--text); font-weight:700; font-size:0.95rem; padding:0.55rem 1.1rem; border-radius:8px; cursor:pointer; transition:all 0.15s; }
  .cbtn:hover { border-color:var(--brand); }
  .cbtn.active { background:var(--brand); border-color:var(--brand); color:#fff; }
  .cresult { background:#fff; border:1px solid var(--border); border-radius:10px; padding:1.25rem; }
  .cresult[hidden] { display:none; }
  .cresult h3 { display:flex; align-items:center; gap:0.5rem; margin:0 0 0.5rem; }
  .cres-actions { display:flex; flex-wrap:wrap; gap:0.75rem; margin-top:0.75rem; }
  .bonus { color:var(--brand); font-size:0.8rem; font-weight:700; }
  .per { font-size:0.8rem; }
  :global(#price-table th.hl), :global(#price-table td.hl) { background:rgba(232,53,47,0.28); }
</style>

<script>
  const buttons = document.querySelectorAll<HTMLButtonElement>('.cbtn');
  const results = document.querySelectorAll<HTMLElement>('.cresult');
  const cells = document.querySelectorAll<HTMLElement>('#price-table [data-col]');
  buttons.forEach((b) =>
    b.addEventListener('click', () => {
      const n = b.dataset.n!;
      buttons.forEach((x) => x.classList.toggle('active', x === b));
      results.forEach((r) => (r.hidden = r.id !== `res-${n}`));
      cells.forEach((c) => c.classList.toggle('hl', c.dataset.col === n));
    })
  );
</script>
